Gource build and installation guide
===================================

This repository contains two native programs:

* the Rust successor, built as the `gource-app` Cargo package; and
* the preserved C++ application, built with the existing autotools/qmake
  files.

The Rust instructions are first. The legacy C++ instructions remain below so
that the original application and its established dependencies are still
available.

Rust native successor
---------------------

Requirements
~~~~~~~~~~~~

The successor is pinned to Rust **1.98.1** in `rust-toolchain.toml`.
`rustup` and Cargo are the supported way to install and use that toolchain:

    rustup toolchain install 1.98.1 --profile minimal --no-self-update

A native `wgpu` adapter and driver are required for an interactive `view` and
for every non-empty frame export. The successor uses native `wgpu` backends
through `winit`; a successful legacy OpenGL run is not evidence for the Rust
renderer on another host.

The optional external tools are:

* `git`, when `--input` names a local repository directory;
* `ffmpeg`, when `export --video` is selected; and
* Python 3 for the archive helper used by the packaging scripts.

Git and FFmpeg are never downloaded or bundled by the application. No runtime
asset or network service is required. FFmpeg remains the user's executable;
the Rust exporter starts it directly with fixed arguments, never through a
shell, and reports a missing executable as an export error.

Build
~~~~~

From the repository root:

    cargo build --locked --release --package gource-app

The binary is `target/release/gource-app` (or
`target/release/gource-app.exe` on Windows). For a development build:

    cargo run --locked -p gource-app -- --help

The six workspace crates are `gource-core`, `gource-ingest`, `gource-sim`,
`gource-render`, `gource-export`, and `gource-app`. The committed `Cargo.lock`
and pinned toolchain are part of a reproducible source checkout.

Run, export, and diagnose
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

The Rust CLI has exactly three subcommands: `view`, `export`, and `diagnose`.
The input is supplied with `--input`; there is no positional-path or legacy
`--log-format` mode in this CLI.

Interactive view:

    ./target/release/gource-app view \
        --input tests/fixtures/visual-hierarchy-activity.log \
        --viewport 1280x720

PNG frame-directory export (FFmpeg is not required):

    ./target/release/gource-app export \
        --input tests/fixtures/single-event.log \
        --output /tmp/gource-frames \
        --viewport 320x180 \
        --frame-rate 60 \
        --start 0 --end 1

FFV1/Matroska export through a user-provided `ffmpeg` on `PATH`:

    ./target/release/gource-app export \
        --input tests/fixtures/single-event.log \
        --output /tmp/gource.mkv --video \
        --frame-rate 60 --start 0 --end 1

Replay diagnostic (no window or GPU context):

    ./target/release/gource-app diagnose \
        --input tests/fixtures/single-event.log --threads 1

`diagnose` emits JSON containing ingest, serial replay, and bounded-parallel
replay timings plus `snapshots_equal`; it exits successfully only when the two
final snapshots compare equal. Use `--threads COUNT` to select the bounded
Rayon worker count and `--end SECONDS` (alias `--target`) to stop both runs at
a repository timestamp. The timings exclude immutable-history cloning and do
not constitute an end-to-end export benchmark.

Input and limits
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

`--input` accepts one of:

* a regular strict custom-log file;
* `-`, meaning finite stdin consumed through EOF; or
* a local directory, which selects the safe built-in Git adapter.

The custom format is
`timestamp|contributor|A|path[|#rrggbb]`. Records are validated, globally
sorted by timestamp and source sequence, and indexed before any history is
published. Input, record, path, contributor, path-depth, event-count, working
memory, temporary-sort disk, and merge fan-in limits are finite and configurable
with the `--max-*`, `--working-*`, and `--run-fan-in` options. A limit failure
never becomes EOF or a silently shortened history.

A directory input is local-only Git ingestion. The adapter validates the
selected directory, snapshots one revision, invokes `git` with explicit
arguments, disables pagers and optional conversion/fetch behavior, bounds
stdout/stderr and working state, and reaps the child on timeout or
cancellation. It does not fetch, contact a remote, execute shell text, or
publish a prefix. A non-repository directory or missing Git executable fails
clearly.

Persistent history caching is opt-in:

    ./target/release/gource-app view \
        --input /tmp/history.log \
        --cache-dir /tmp/private-gource-cache \
        --cache-bytes 1073741824

The application caches regular custom-log files only. Stdin and Git-directory
inputs bypass the persistent cache. The default aggregate entry quota is 4 GiB
when caching is enabled; `--cache-bytes` must be finite and non-zero. Cache
roots are created owner-private and entries are checksummed, validated, and
atomically published. See `docs/cache.md` and `docs/security.md` for the
privacy, quota, eviction, and failure rules.

Configuration
~~~~~~~~~~~~~

Select one TOML file with `--config FILE`, or set `GOURCE_CONFIG` when the
option is absent. Precedence is built-in defaults, TOML, known `GOURCE_*`
environment variables, then explicit command-line options. Paths remain
relative to the process working directory. The accepted keys and command
sections are documented in `docs/configuration.md`; old C++ `.conf`, `.cfg`,
and `.ini` files are not silently loaded by the Rust application.

Native packaging
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

The target wrappers build with the pinned toolchain, run packaged `--help` and
`diagnose` checks, validate required archive entries, and write a SHA-256
sidecar. They do not claim interactive GPU or FFmpeg support beyond the target
smoke evidence that has actually been recorded.

Linux x86_64:

    bash packaging/linux-x86_64.sh --output-dir dist

macOS Intel or Apple Silicon:

    bash packaging/macos.sh --target x86_64-apple-darwin --output-dir dist
    bash packaging/macos.sh --target arm64-apple-darwin --output-dir dist

Windows PowerShell:

    .\packaging\windows-x86_64.ps1 -OutputDirectory dist

Each archive contains `bin/gource-app`, `COPYING`,
`THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES`, the README and man page, `data/gource.style`, the font
notice, and the finite single-event fixture. FFmpeg, Git, the unresolved
FreeSans binary, and uncleared legacy sprites are not bundled. See
`docs/releasing.md` for corresponding-source and platform-evidence rules.

Legacy C++ application
----------------------

Dependencies
~~~~~~~~~~~~

The preserved C++ program requires the following libraries to compile (package
names vary by distribution):

    SDL 2.0 (libsdl2-dev)
    SDL Image 2.0 (libsdl2-image-dev)
    PCRE2 (libpcre2-dev)
    Freetype 2 (libfreetype6-dev)
    GLEW (libglew-dev)
    GLM >= 0.9.3 (libglm-dev)
    Boost Filesystem >= 1.69 (libboost-filesystem-dev)
    PNG >= 1.2 (libpng-dev)

Optional:

    TinyXML (libtinyxml-dev)

SDL Image 2.0 must have support for PNG and JPEG images enabled.

Building
~~~~~~~~

The legacy build requires a GNU-compatible C++ compiler with C++0x features
such as `auto` and range-based `for` loops. GCC 4.6+ or Clang is recommended.

When building from a checkout of the legacy Git tree, first generate the
configure script and initialize/update its submodules:

    ./autogen.sh

Generic Linux/macOS autotools build:

    ./configure
    make
    make install

Windows legacy build:

Compile `gource.pro` with Qt Creator. A pre-built C++ version has historically
been published from the homepage, but that is separate from the Rust
`gource-app` package.

The legacy program expects SDL to have been built with the
`NO_STDIO_REDIRECT` flag.

Configure options
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

By default the legacy program installs GNU FreeFont TTF fonts. If the fonts
are already installed, point configure at them:

    ./configure --enable-ttf-font-dir=/path/to/freefont/

A different FreeType-supported font can be selected at build time:

    ./configure --enable-font-file=/path/to/alternate/font.ttf

The legacy tree includes a copy of TinyXML. To build against the system
library:

    ./configure --with-tinyxml

The legacy dependency list and options above do not change the Rust CLI.
